---
title: Tomato pasta, tuned
description: Lesson 6.3. Adding depth with umami, and softening sharpness with fat.
servings: 2
time: 35 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, tasting-and-fixing]
lesson: "6.3"
technique: adjusting umami and fat
status: draft
---

> Technique: adding depth, then softening. Salt and acid adjust a dish
> along two lines. This lesson adds two more: umami, the savoury taste,
> and fat. A plain tomato sauce is the test bed.

Put @olive oil{2%tbsp} and @garlic{2%cloves}(thinly sliced) in a cold #saucepan{} over low heat for ~{2%minutes}, until the garlic is soft. Add @anchovy fillets{2} and mash them with a #wooden spoon{} until they melt into the oil.

Add @tinned tomatoes{400%g}, crush them with the spoon, and simmer for ~{15%minutes}, until thickened.

> Science: umami is the taste of glutamate, identified in the seaweed
> kombu by Kikunae Ikeda in 1908. Tomato has 150 to 250 mg of free
> glutamate per 100 g and cheese 300 to 1,680 mg. A second group
> of compounds, the nucleotides inosinate and guanylate, multiply it:
> in people, glutamate mixed with inosinate gives about 8 times the
> response of glutamate alone. One of its receptors is T1R1 + T1R3.
> Look up:
> umami, glutamate, inosinate, umami synergy.
-- source: Kurihara, "Umami the Fifth Basic Taste: History of Studies on Receptor Mechanisms and Role as a Food Flavor", BioMed Research International 2015:189402, https://doi.org/10.1155/2015/189402 (inosinate is found only in animal foods, and the review's example is dried bonito; the anchovy here is this recipe's animal source on that reasoning, not a measured one)

Meanwhile, bring @water{2%litres} to a boil in a #large pot{} with @salt{10%g} and cook @penne{200%g} for the time on the packet.

Taste the sauce. Add @fine salt{1/2%tsp} a pinch at a time, tasting between pinches, until the tomato tastes full.

Taste again for sharpness. If the sauce is sharp, stir in @butter{15%g} and taste. Only if it is still sharp, add @sugar{1/4%tsp}.

> Why fat before sugar: both soften sourness. Fat coats the tongue;
> sugar balances it, and a sauce pushed too far that way just tastes
> sweet.
-- source: Rebecca Hays, "Is Your Dish Too Salty? Sweet? Sour? Spicy? Here's How to Fix It.", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-07-04, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5816-is-your-dish-too-salty-sweet-sour-spicy-here's-how-to-fix-it (too sour: "a fat such as butter ... to coat the tongue", or a sweetener)

Scoop out a cupful of pasta water and drain the pasta. Toss the pasta with the sauce, @parmesan{30%g}(finely grated) and 2 tablespoons of the pasta water. If the sauce is thick and pasty, loosen it with more pasta water before you reach for more salt.

> Science: thickness changes flavour. In an experiment with model
> sauces where the amount of aroma released was held constant, people
> still rated the flavour weaker as the liquid got thicker in the
> mouth. So a sauce that tastes dull may need thinning, not seasoning.
> Look up: flavour release, viscosity, fat in flavour perception.
-- source: Bayarri, Taylor and Hort, "The Role of Fat in Flavor Perception: Effect of Partition and Viscosity in Model Emulsions", Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry 54(23):8862-8868, 2006, https://doi.org/10.1021/jf061537k

> Next time, without the recipe: when a dish is properly salted and
> still thin in flavour, add something rich in glutamate (tomato,
> cheese, kombu) and something that carries inosinate or guanylate
> (dried fish, dried mushrooms). When it is sharp, add fat first.
